# sortnetopt-endgame-v3 — env-gated "endgame" successor filters (E1, E2, E4)
#
# Applies on top of the eleven-patch v3 stack, in this order:
#
#     sortnetopt-macos-proc.patch
#     sortnetopt-instrumentation-v3.patch
#     sortnetopt-online-subsumption-v3.patch
#     sortnetopt-perf-v3.patch
#     sortnetopt-tier1-v3.patch
#     sortnetopt-tier2a-v3.patch
#     sortnetopt-tier2b-v3.patch
#     sortnetopt-limits-v3.patch
#     sortnetopt-zm-v3.patch
#     sortnetopt-decomp-v3.patch
#     sortnetopt-prefixcert-v3.patch
#     sortnetopt-endgame-v3.patch     <- this one
#
# Base pin: 0b5d09c47446096f9e3a0812b35afc72b7f2a718.
# Apply with:  patch -V none -p1 -i <this file>
#
# checker/ is NOT touched. Neither are evidence/, config/, ledger/, or the
# pinned third_party clone. Three files change:
#
#     src/search.rs             successor-expansion loop in `Search::improve`;
#                                `Edges`/`EdgeTarget` gain an `endgame_kept`
#                                flag and `add_edge` an `endgame_kept: bool`
#                                parameter (every existing call site updated,
#                                including the unrelated `improve_huffman`
#                                extremal-pruning edges, which always pass
#                                `true`)
#     src/instrument.rs          new counters, two 64-bucket histograms, and
#                                their JSON emission
#     src/search/endgame.rs (new) the three filter functions, the env-var
#                                config, and unit tests
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# REFERENCE MODEL
#
# tools/verify_endgame.py is the authority for the semantics ported here
# (functions `f_bound1_unique`, `f_halving`; the third filter's definition was
# corrected after that file was written -- see E3/E4 below). Nothing in that
# file or this patch touches the engine's search *result*: every filter is
# proven to retain at least one bound-optimal successor whenever it is
# consulted, and the empty-admissible-set case (which would otherwise be
# possible if a filter were unsound, or if its structural precondition failed
# to hold) always falls back to the unfiltered successor set rather than ever
# risk excluding every optimal successor.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE GATE
#
# Filters are only consulted for a state whose *parent* has a stored upper
# bound `b = state.bounds[1] <= SORTNETOPT_ENDGAME_BUDGET` (default 4). `b` is
# always a sound upper bound on the true `bound(X)`, never required to be
# tight: if the true value is `m <= b`, any optimal successor Y has
# `bound(Y) = m - 1 <= b - 1`, so a test parameterised by `b - 1` can never
# exclude every optimal successor. This is the soundness argument for all
# three filters; see `src/search/endgame.rs`'s module doc comment for the
# per-filter detail.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE THREE FILTERS
#
# E1 (bound1_unique_pair). When b == 1 and X is unsorted, bound(X) is exactly
# 1 (not just <= 1 -- state.bounds[0] >= 1 for every unsorted state in this
# engine's memo table, from its very first synthesized default, so
# b == 1 == state.bounds[0] forces bound(X) == 1 exactly), and the sorting
# comparator is uniquely determined by X's Hamming-weight class structure:
# every weight class has size <= 2; every size-2 class differs in exactly the
# same two channels {i, j}; that pair is the comparator. When this holds, the
# whole `i, j` double loop `Search::improve` normally runs is replaced by
# trying that one pair -- `SUCCESSORS_GENERATED`-equivalent work drops from
# O(channels^2) to O(1), and every other pair's `canonicalize` + memo
# lookup/insert is skipped outright.
#
#   EMPIRICAL NOTE, not merely theoretical: across every acceptance run of this
#   patch (n = 7, 8, 9), `endgame_e1_states` measured exactly 0. This is not a
#   bug; see "SUSPICIOUS / NOTEWORTHY" below for the structural argument for
#   why the b == 1 gate looks unreachable in this specific engine's control
#   flow, and why the implementation is kept (and tested in isolation) anyway.
#
# E2 (halving_ok). A comparator is at most 2-to-1 within a Hamming weight
# class, so bound(X) >= ceil(log2 max_l |X_l|). A successor Y (post-
# apply_comparator, pre-canonicalize) is admissible only if
# max_l |Y_l| <= 2^(b-1).
#
# E3/E4 (live_support / live_budget_ok). CORRECTED MID-IMPLEMENTATION: the
# original spec's "dirty channel = incident to an unsettled pair" definition
# turned out to be an unproven conjecture. The definition actually implemented
# is LIVE SUPPORT, which has the following proof:
#
#   L(Z) = { p : exist u, v in Z, popcount(u) == popcount(v), u_p != v_p }
#        = OR over Hamming weights l of ( OR_l(Z) & !AND_l(Z) )
#          where OR_l / AND_l are the bitwise OR / AND of every vector of
#          weight l present in Z (viewed as a `channels`-bit value).
#
#   Proof that every channel in L(Z) is touched by every network sorting Z:
#   comparator networks preserve Hamming weight, and `OutputSet::is_sorted`
#   accepts Z iff Z has at most one vector per weight. If network N never
#   touches channel p, N(v)_p = v_p for every v. Take u, v in Z of equal
#   weight with u_p != v_p (exactly what p in L(Z) means); N(u) and N(v) then
#   have equal weight too, so if N(Z) is sorted, N(u) = N(v) -- but they still
#   disagree at channel p, a contradiction. Hence every channel of L(Z) is
#   touched by some comparator of N, and each comparator touches exactly 2
#   channels, so bound(Z) >= ceil(|L(Z)| / 2).
#
#   As a filter: successor Y is admissible only if |L(Y)| <= 2 * (b - 1).
#
#   This is computed in one O(2^channels) pass over the bitmap (per-weight
#   OR/AND, then one more pass combining them), independent of |X| -- cheaper
#   than the "unsettled pair" formulation would have been (which needs an
#   O(channels^2 * 2^channels) scan via per-pair quadrant counts).
#
# Composite policy (mirrors verify_endgame.py's f_e1_e2_e4): at b == 1, only
# E1 applies and replaces enumeration outright; at b >= 2, E2 and E4 both
# apply to each successor the ordinary double loop finds.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# EMPTY-ADMISSIBLE-SET HANDLING (spec item 4): FALLBACK, not lower-bound bump
#
# `Search::improve`'s fixpoint loop does
# `edges.states().map(|s| s.bounds[i]).min().unwrap()`, which panics on an
# empty `Edges`. Before this patch that was unreachable (an unsorted set
# always has a non-redundant comparator); a filter that excludes every
# candidate could make it reachable. Per the task's explicit preference
# ("prefer the fallback if there is any doubt"), this patch ALWAYS falls back
# to the full unfiltered double loop rather than attempt any in-place lower-
# bound bookkeeping:
#
#   * E1: if `bound1_unique_pair` returns `None` (the weight-class
#     precondition failed -- expected never to happen given a correct b == 1
#     gate, but not asserted), the fast path is abandoned and the ordinary
#     double loop below runs unfiltered.
#   * E2/E4: the filtered attempt tracks whether *any* successor was kept
#     (locally, not via the shared `Edges`); if none was, the full unfiltered
#     double loop is re-run from scratch.
#
# Both paths bump `endgame_empty_fallback`. It measured 0 in every acceptance
# run.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# AUDIT MODE (SORTNETOPT_ENDGAME_AUDIT)
#
# Builds the unfiltered successor set always (so the search's actual answer
# never depends on the filters in audit mode), while also recording -- per
# `(i, j)` pair, aggregated per canonical target via `EdgeTarget::endgame_kept`
# -- which successors the filters would have kept. At every iteration of the
# fixpoint loop in `Search::improve`, checks that
# `min(bounds[0] over kept) == min(bounds[0] over all)`; a mismatch bumps the
# shared `FILTER_AUDIT_VIOLATIONS` counter (the same one TIER1 / Lemma M
# already use) and logs an error with the packed set. Measured 0 in every
# acceptance run, including a full n = 9 --limit 25 run.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# INSTRUMENTATION (src/instrument.rs)
#
# Scalar counters (all AtomicU64, Relaxed, incremented outside any lock):
# endgame_expansions_total, endgame_gate_reached, endgame_e1_states,
# endgame_e1_pairs_skipped, endgame_e2_rejected, endgame_e4_rejected,
# endgame_empty_fallback; plus config echoes endgame_enabled,
# endgame_budget_cap, endgame_audit_enabled.
#
# Two 64-bucket histograms, `endgame_upper_hist` / `endgame_lower_hist`
# (indexed by the parent's `bounds[1]` / `bounds[0]`, clamped to the top
# bucket), recorded UNCONDITIONALLY at every successor-expansion event
# whenever SORTNETOPT_ENDGAME is set (regardless of whether the budget gate
# itself fires) and always emitted in the JSON dump (all-zero when the flag is
# unset). This is the headline measurement of the whole feature: what
# fraction of the search's successor expansions are anywhere near the sorted
# end. See the report accompanying this patch for the measured n = 9
# --limit 25 array.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# ENVIRONMENT VARIABLES INTRODUCED
#
#   SORTNETOPT_ENDGAME             master switch (default off). Unset, no
#                                   function in src/search/endgame.rs is ever
#                                   called and the successor loop is byte-for-
#                                   byte the pre-patch code.
#   SORTNETOPT_ENDGAME_BUDGET       the `b <= budget_cap` gate (default 4).
#   SORTNETOPT_ENDGAME_AUDIT        audit mode (default off); see above.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# SUSPICIOUS / NOTEWORTHY: E1's gate looks structurally unreachable here
#
# `Search::improve` returns immediately, before any successor generation, once
# `state.bounds[0] == state.bounds[1]`. Every unsorted state's `bounds[0]` is
# seeded to (and never drops below) 1 from its very first synthesized default
# in `StateMap::get`. Therefore `state.bounds[1] == 1` for a state that reaches
# the successor-generation code would require `state.bounds[0] < 1`, which
# cannot happen -- so `state.bounds[0] == state.bounds[1] == 1` and the early
# return already fired, in this call or an earlier one (both the fixpoint
# loop's own `combined_upper_bound` assignment and the extremal-channel-
# pruning path re-check `bounds[0] == bounds[1]` immediately after writing
# `bounds[1]`, so bounds[1] can never visibly reach 1 without an immediate
# return). Measured: `endgame_e1_states == 0` in every one of this patch's
# acceptance runs (n = 7, 8, 9, several seeds each). E1 is implemented and
# unit-tested in isolation regardless -- it is a correct, sound filter, simply
# one this particular engine's control flow appears never to hand a live
# opportunity to fire on. This is worth an independent look before relying on
# it in a lower-memory / different-structured search variant where the b == 1
# gate might actually be reachable.
#
